# The notebook places record 719 before 718.
talk(719,'1987-10-19','I. M. Gelfand','P001395-P001396','Summation formulas and a parabolic double-coset algebra',r'Gelfand derives a triangular system relating derivative sums to endpoint differences, leading to the Euler--Maclaurin formula. The same short contribution recalls the Coxeter presentation of a symmetric group and introduces the subalgebra invariant on both sides under a Young subgroup. The second topic ends at the top of P001396, before Beilinson resumes.')
item(719,'P001395','method','Taylor telescoping and universal summation coefficients',r'The sums \(S_r=\sum_{j=1}^n f^{(r)}(j)\) are expressed by a triangular system involving endpoint differences, with a determinant solution.',r'For a polynomial \(f\) and an antiderivative \(F\), \[F(n+1)-F(1)=\sum_{r\ge0}\frac{S_r}{(r+1)!},\qquad f^{(p)}(n+1)-f^{(p)}(1)=\sum_{r\ge1}\frac{S_{p+r}}{r!}.\] All sums terminate, and the unit triangular system can be inverted.',r'This is a direct polynomial version of the classical Euler--Maclaurin method. \citet[\S 2.10(i), equation 2.10.1]{nist2026dlmf} states the analytic formula with its remainder.','the polynomial identity is exact; applying an infinite Taylor expansion to an arbitrary function is unjustified.', ['nist2026dlmf'],reconstruction='Polynomial input supplies a rigorous domain for the notebook\textquotesingle s formal manipulation.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Analysis and summation')
item(719,'P001395','theorem','Bernoulli coefficients and Euler--Maclaurin summation',r'The universal coefficients are recovered using exponentials; the notes propose the answer for any function.',r'Writing \(t/(e^t-1)=\sum_{r\ge0}B_rt^r/r!\), a polynomial \(f=F^{\prime}\) satisfies \[\sum_{j=1}^nf(j)=F(n+1)-F(1)+\sum_{r\ge1}\frac{B_r}{r!}\bigl(f^{(r-1)}(n+1)-f^{(r-1)}(1)\bigr).\] For nonpolynomial functions one uses a finite truncation with the Euler--Maclaurin remainder and sufficient differentiability.',r'\citet[equations 24.2.1 and 2.10.1]{nist2026dlmf} verifies the generating function, sign \(B_1=-1/2\), and remainder. This was classical long before 1987.','the coefficient sequence is established; the unrestricted no-remainder statement is false.', ['nist2026dlmf'],reconstruction='The exact polynomial case is separated from the analytic finite-remainder theorem.',literal='false as written',development='annotated',area='Analysis and summation')
item(719,'P001395-P001396','definition','Symmetric-group generators and the Young-subgroup algebra',r'The simple transpositions obey Coxeter relations; for \(H=S_k\times S_{n-k}\), the algebra consists of \(x\in\mathbb C[S_n]\) satisfying \(hx=xh=x\) for all \(h\in H\).',r'The group \(S_n\) is generated by \(s_i=(i,i+1)\), with \(s_i^2=1\), \(s_is_j=s_js_i\) for \(|i-j|>1\), and \(s_is_{i+1}s_i=s_{i+1}s_is_{i+1}\). For \(e_H=|H|^{-1}\sum_{h\in H}h\), the displayed invariant algebra is \(e_H\mathbb C[S_n]e_H\), with unit \(e_H\); it has a basis indexed by \(H\backslash S_n/H\).',r'This is the ordinary finite-group double-coset construction, related to the symmetric-group representation framework of \citet[Chapter I, \S 7]{macdonald1995symmetric}. No further theorem or unanswered question about this algebra is recorded here.','the definition is complete after making its unit explicit.', [M],reconstruction='The averaging idempotent identifies the algebra and clarifies that its unit need not be the ambient group-algebra unit.',match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Representation theory')
corr('P001395','last derivative telescoping row','Endpoint difference of f^(p) begins with S_p','It begins with S_(p+1)','Differentiating order p and Taylor expanding f^(p)(j+1)-f^(p)(j) makes the first term f^(p+1)(j); the scan confirms the printed index.')
corr('P001395','exponential generating function','((1/(n+1)) exp(t(n+1))-e)/(exp(t)-1)','sum_{j=1}^n exp(tj)=(exp(t(n+1))-exp(t))/(exp(t)-1)','Direct geometric-series identity; the notebook fraction is inconsistent even at n=1. Treat as a mathematical emendation, not a silent transcription correction.')
corr('P001395','scope of summation formula','For any functions','For polynomials the finite formula is exact; other functions require convergence hypotheses or a finite Euler--Maclaurin remainder','NIST DLMF 2.10.1 supplies explicit analytic assumptions and remainder.')
checkpoint()

talk(718,'1987-10-19','A. A. Beilinson','P001396-P001398','Truncation, mapping cones, and distinguished triangles',r'Beilinson continues the derived-category course after Gelfand\textquotesingle s intervention. He explains how cohomology in one degree identifies a complex in the derived category, contrasts this with splitting in several degrees, and introduces mapping cones and exact triangles. Several signs and the ordering of cone summands require explicit conventions.')
item(718,'P001396','construction','Truncation and a complex with one cohomology object',r'The complex is cut off using a kernel, and, when only its zeroth cohomology survives, it is compared with that object in degree zero.',r'If \(H^i(X)=0\) for \(i\ne0\), the canonical truncation \(\tau_{\le0}X\), with \((\tau_{\le0}X)^0=\ker d^0\), has quasi-isomorphisms \(X\leftarrow\tau_{\le0}X\to H^0(X)[0]\). Hence \(X\simeq H^0(X)[0]\) in \(D(\mathcal A)\).',r'This is standard truncation in the derived category, already part of \citet[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues}; compare \citet[Chapter 13]{stacks2026foundations}.','the conclusion is canonical in the derived category and does not imply a chain-level isomorphism.',[H,S],area='Sheaves and homological algebra')
item(718,'P001396-P001397','claim','Cohomology objects do not generally split a complex',r'The notes warn that several cohomology groups do not determine a complex as their direct sum, and that a splitting, when available, need not be canonical.',r'In a general abelian category a complex need not be isomorphic in \(D(\mathcal A)\) to its graded cohomology with zero differential: the truncation triangles can have nonzero connecting extension classes. If every short exact sequence in \(\mathcal A\) splits, every complex is homotopy equivalent to its graded cohomology, although choices enter a chain-level splitting.',r'The distinction is built into the triangle formalism of \citet[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues} and \citet[\S\S 13.9--13.11]{stacks2026foundations}.','nonformality is a real phenomenon; the semisimple hypothesis is sufficient for the stated splitting.',[H,S],reconstruction='The extension-class explanation specifies the warning rather than imputing a particular example to the speaker.',match='partial',area='Sheaves and homological algebra')
item(718,'P001397','problem','When is the homotopy category abelian?',r'The exercise says \(K(\mathcal A)\) is abelian exactly when \(\mathcal A\) is semisimple; the handwritten explanation speaks of sums of irreducibles.',r'For an abelian category \(\mathcal A\), \(K(\mathcal A)\) is abelian if and only if every short exact sequence in \(\mathcal A\) splits. In that case it is equivalent to the category of graded objects of \(\mathcal A\). Here semisimple means split exact; a decomposition into simple objects requires the usual additional finiteness or category hypotheses.',r'The implication from split exactness follows by splitting cycles and boundaries. Conversely an abelian triangulated category is split exact, so every morphism has a generalized inverse. For a monomorphism \(i:U\to V\) in \(\mathcal A\), apply this to its degree-zero map to obtain \(g\) with \(igi=i\); cancellation in \(\mathcal A\) gives \(gi=1_U\), forcing all exact sequences in \(\mathcal A\) to split. The triangulated structure is checked in \citet[Proposition 13.10.3]{stacks2026foundations}.','the exercise is a settled structural criterion, with the meaning of semisimple specified.',[S,H],reconstruction='The split-exact formulation avoids silently imposing finite length.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',development='annotated',area='Sheaves and homological algebra')
item(718,'P001397-P001398','construction','Mapping cone and its elementary homotopies',r'The cone is \(C_f^i=Y^i\oplus X^{i+1}\), but the differential is written with the reversed coordinate ordering. The cone of the identity is contractible; the cone of zero splits.',r'For a chain map \(f:X\to Y\), put \(C_f^i=Y^i\oplus X^{i+1}\) and \(d(y,x)=(d_Yy+f(x),-d_Xx)\). Then \(C_{1_X}\) is contractible and \(C_0=Y\oplus X[1]\). If \(i:Y\to C_f\) is the inclusion, \(C_i\) is homotopy equivalent to \(X[1]\).',r'\citet[\S 13.9, definition and elementary cone lemmas]{stacks2026foundations} gives the standard construction. These statements were established well before the seminar \citep[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues}.','all three cone properties are valid with consistent shift and summand conventions.',[S,H],reconstruction='The differential has been reordered to match the declared direct sum.',literal='false as written',area='Sheaves and homological algebra')
item(718,'P001397-P001398','definition','Distinguished triangles and the sign of rotation',r'Exact triangles are those isomorphic to cone triangles, followed by a continuing sequence of shifts.',r'The triangle \(X\xrightarrow fY\to C_f\to X[1]\), with one consistent cone convention, is distinguished; all triangles isomorphic to such ones are distinguished. If \(X\xrightarrow fY\xrightarrow gZ\xrightarrow hX[1]\) is distinguished, its rotation is \(Y\xrightarrow gZ\xrightarrow hX[1]\xrightarrow{-f[1]}Y[1]\). Its cohomology sequence is exact.',r'\citet[Proposition 13.10.3, proof of TR2, and equation 13.11.1.1]{stacks2026foundations} checks the rotation sign and long exact sequence. The 1966 published account predates this exposition \citep[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues}.','the rotation sign is indispensable even though signs can be moved by coherent changes of cone convention.',[S,H],reconstruction='The negative sign on the rotated final arrow is made explicit; the notebook\textquotesingle s continuing sequence alone does not state a sign convention.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Sheaves and homological algebra')
corr('P001397','mapping-cone differential','C_f^i=Y^i direct sum X^(i+1), d(x,y)=(-dx,dy+fx)','With the declared ordering, d(y,x)=(d_Y y+f x,-d_X x)','The scan and Russian text combine opposite orderings; direct verification of d squared=0 fixes the consistent form.')
