talk(720,'1987-11-09','I. M. Gelfand','P001399-P001405','Symmetric functions and Schubert polynomials after Lascoux',r'Gelfand develops symmetric functions in alphabet notation, determinant identities, and Schubert polynomials via divided differences. The finite coinvariant ring, its pairing, and the symmetric-group action connect these formulas with flag cohomology. The final request for a character interpretation had already been answered in the appropriate Borel-module category by the 1986 preprint and 1987 announcement of Kra\'skiewicz and Pragacz; the notebook\textquotesingle s word irreducible needs qualification.')
item(720,'P001399','definition','Complete functions of a formal alphabet',r'For a formal sum \(P\) the coefficients \(S^r(P)\) are defined by a product generating function, with examples allowing multiplicities and differences of alphabets.',r'For a finite alphabet with multiplicities \(P=\sum_jm_ju_j\), \(m_j\in\mathbb Z\), define \[\sum_{r\ge0}h_r[P]t^r=\prod_j(1-tu_j)^{-m_j},\qquad h_0[P]=1,\quad h_r[P]=0\ (r<0).\] Positive multiplicities give ordinary complete symmetric functions; negative ones define virtual alphabets formally.',r'This is classical symmetric-function notation; \citet[Chapter I, \S 2]{macdonald1995symmetric}. The first edition appeared in 1979.','the construction is a formal power-series identity; no analytic convergence is required.',[M],area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001399-P001400','theorem','The alphabet homomorphism to triangular Toeplitz matrices',r'The matrix \(M(P)\) has consecutive complete functions along diagonals and is called Hankel; the notes assert \(M(P+Q)=M(P)M(Q)\).',r'Let \(M(P)_{ij}=h_{j-i}[P]\) for \(i,j\ge0\). Then \(M(P)\) is an upper triangular Toeplitz matrix with diagonal one and \(M(P+Q)=M(P)M(Q)\), since \(h_r[P+Q]=\sum_{a+b=r}h_a[P]h_b[Q]\). Every matrix entry involves a finite sum.',r'The identity follows by multiplying the generating series in \citet[Chapter I, \S 2]{macdonald1995symmetric}. The matrix is Toeplitz, since its entries depend on the difference of the indices.','the homomorphism is correct; the notebook\textquotesingle s matrix-name label is incorrect.',[M],literal='false as written',development='annotated',area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001400','theorem','Cauchy--Binet through exterior powers',r'The notes use minors of a matrix and the multiplicativity of its exterior powers.',r'For compatible finite matrices \(A,B\), and ordered \(k\)-element index sets \(I,J\), \[\det(AB)_{I,J}=\sum_{|L|=k}\det A_{I,L}\det B_{L,J}.\] Equivalently \(\bigwedge^k(AB)=(\bigwedge^kA)(\bigwedge^kB)\); the same identity applies to the triangular matrices here because relevant sums are finite.',r'The identity is classical multilinear algebra and underlies the determinant manipulations in \citet[Chapter I, \S\S 3--5]{macdonald1995symmetric}.','the equality is valid over every commutative coefficient ring.',[M],match='partial',area='Linear algebra')
item(720,'P001400','definition','Skew Schur functions as minors',r'Minors of \(M(P)\), indexed by two ordered integer lists, are denoted \(S_{I\mid J}(P)\).',r'For partitions \(\mu\subseteq\lambda\), padded to a common length \(r\), the skew Schur function is \[s_{\lambda/\mu}[P]=\det\bigl(h_{\lambda_i-\mu_j-i+j}[P]\bigr)_{1\le i,j\le r}.\] Reversing the notebook\textquotesingle s increasing index order gives this usual partition notation.',r'\citet[Chapter I, \S 5, skew Jacobi--Trudi identities]{macdonald1995symmetric}.','these minors give the standard skew Schur functions; an explicit index reversal is necessary when comparing formulas.',[M],reconstruction='The partition convention is modern; it does not replace the notebook\textquotesingle s increasing-code convention without an index change.',area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001400-P001401','theorem','Alternants and the Jacobi--Trudi identity',r'The notes define Schur polynomials by a quotient of alternants and ask for the determinant identity relating this quotient to complete functions.',r'For a partition \(\lambda\) of length at most \(n\), \[s_\lambda(x_1,\ldots,x_n)=\frac{\det(x_i^{\lambda_j+n-j})}{\det(x_i^{n-j})}=\det(h_{\lambda_i-i+j}(x_1,\ldots,x_n)).\] The quotient is a symmetric polynomial with integral coefficients, since the alternating numerator is divisible by the Vandermonde.',r'\citet[Chapter I, \S 3, equation (3.4) and historical notes]{macdonald1995symmetric} attributes the determinant identity to Jacobi.','the requested proof concerns a classical exact identity.',[M],area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001400-P001401','question','Schur coefficients and their representation meaning',r'The text asks for explicit coefficients and a representation-theoretic interpretation; an abbreviated reference to a Gelfand--Zelevinsky 1986 paper is attached.',r'In characteristic zero, \(s_\lambda\) is the character of the Schur functor \(S_\lambda V\) for \(\operatorname{GL}(V)\). In \(s_\lambda=\sum_\mu K_{\lambda\mu}m_\mu\), \(K_{\lambda\mu}\) counts semistandard tableaux of shape \(\lambda\) and content \(\mu\). These give a precise coefficient interpretation, though not a single factorial expression for all coefficients.',r'The character and tableau descriptions predate 1987; \citet[Chapter I, \S 5 and Appendix A]{macdonald1995symmetric}. The notebook\textquotesingle s terse Gelfand--Zelevinsky bibliographic pointer could not be matched securely to a particular theorem and is not used as evidence of priority.','the ordinary Schur question has an established answer; the exact intended 1986 citation remains unresolved.',[M],reconstruction='The coefficient basis and the polynomial representation category are supplied explicitly.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',area='Representation theory')
item(720,'P001400','remark','The K-functor and Sato comparison',r'A parenthetical passage connects the determinant formalism with a K-functor and Sato; no operator, category, or assertion is defined there.',r'No uniquely specified mathematical statement can be recovered from this aside: neither the K-functor nor the proposed Sato correspondence is defined in the supplied pages.',r'Targeted searches for the alphabet determinant formalism and Sato theory located related symmetric-function literature but no exact result identifiable from the fragment. The surrounding determinant identities remain independently identified.','this is a provenance and interpretation gap, not an established unsolved theorem.',[],cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Integrable systems')
item(720,'P001401','claim','Plücker relations as the determinant background',r'The note connects the identities among minors with Grassmannians and Plücker relations.',r'For a rank-\(r\) matrix, its ordered maximal minors \(p_I\) satisfy the quadratic Pl\"ucker relations \[\sum_{a=1}^{r+1}(-1)^a p_{i_1\cdots i_{r-1}j_a}\,p_{j_1\cdots\widehat{j_a}\cdots j_{r+1}}=0,\] with each index list interpreted alternately. These are the defining equations of the Pl\"ucker embedded Grassmannian.',r'This is the classical determinant geometry also used in \citet[\S 1]{gelfandserganova1987combinatorial}; the notebook returns to the exchange identity in record 725.','the relation is established; no new higher Plücker constraint is specified here.', ['gelfandserganova1987combinatorial'],reconstruction='The index convention and ambient Grassmannian have been made explicit.',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(720,'P001401','theorem','Addition and multiplication of alphabets',r'Besides the addition formula for complete functions, the notes expand a complete function of the product alphabet in products of Schur functions.',r'For finite alphabets \(X=(x_i)\), \(Y=(y_j)\), \[h_n[X+Y]=\sum_{a+b=n}h_a[X]h_b[Y],\qquad h_n[XY]=\sum_{\lambda\vdash n}s_\lambda[X]s_\lambda[Y].\] Here \(XY\) is the alphabet \((x_iy_j)\); Schur functions with too many rows specialize to zero.',r'\citet[Chapter I, \S 4, Cauchy identity]{macdonald1995symmetric}.','both are exact polynomial identities, valid for virtual alphabets by formal specialization.',[M],area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001401','construction','Plethysm and its Schur expansion',r'The notes form complete functions of an alphabet whose entries are themselves monomials and write an expansion in Schur functions.',r'For positive integers \(m,n\), \(h_n[h_m]=\sum_{\lambda\vdash mn}c_\lambda s_\lambda\), where \(c_\lambda\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}\) is the multiplicity of \(S_\lambda V\) in \(\operatorname{Sym}^n(\operatorname{Sym}^mV)\) in characteristic zero and sufficiently large dimension. Plethysm is defined by substitution of the eigenvalue alphabet.',r'\citet[Chapter I, \S 8, especially (8.2) and (8.10)]{macdonald1995symmetric}. The notes state the operation and expansion, not a quantified positivity conjecture or a uniform combinatorial rule.','the existence and nonnegativity of this expansion are classical; the fragment does not justify entering a modern plethysm open problem.',[M],reconstruction='The characteristic-zero representation interpretation explains the coefficients without claiming an explicit rule absent from the notes.',area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001401-P001402','theorem','Flag cohomology and Schubert classes',r'Schubert cells are compared with differential forms and a polynomial presentation of flag cohomology.',r'For the complete flag manifold \(\operatorname{Fl}(\mathbb C^N)\), \[H^*(\operatorname{Fl}(\mathbb C^N),\mathbb Z)\simeq\mathbb Z[x_1,\ldots,x_N]/(e_1,\ldots,e_N),\qquad\deg x_i=2.\] Schubert classes form an integral basis; their indices are permutations and their complex codimensions are inversion lengths.',r'\citet[\S\S 2--3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert} treats the polynomial and Schubert descriptions. Its 1973 publication precedes this seminar. See earlier occurrences 1971-239.1--2 and 1985-661.3.','the cohomology is torsion free and concentrated in even real degrees.',[B],area='Algebraic geometry')
item(720,'P001402','definition','Divided differences and nil-Coxeter relations',r'The operator \(\partial_i=(1-s_i)/(x_i-x_{i+1})\) is introduced, with nilpotence, commuting, and braid relations.',r'On \(\mathbb Z[x_1,x_2,\ldots]\), define \(\partial_if=(f-s_if)/(x_i-x_{i+1})\). Then \(\partial_i^2=0\), \(\partial_i\partial_j=\partial_j\partial_i\) for \(|i-j|>1\), and \(\partial_i\partial_{i+1}\partial_i=\partial_{i+1}\partial_i\partial_{i+1}\). If \(g\) is \(s_i\)-invariant then \(\partial_i(gf)=g\partial_if\).',r'The divided-difference formalism appears in \citet[\S 3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert}; the later primary Schubert-module paper recalls it in \citet[\S 1]{kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert}.','the operators are integral because the numerator is divisible by the displayed linear factor.',[B,'kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert'],area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001402','remark','The Hecke deformation of the quadratic relation',r'Alongside the nilpotent relation, the notes mention the quadratic relation \((\sigma_i+1)(\sigma_i-q)=0\) as its q-analogue.',r'The type-A Hecke algebra is generated by \(T_i\) with braid and distant-commutation relations and \((T_i-q)(T_i+1)=0\). This is a deformation of the symmetric-group presentation at \(q=1\). The divided-difference operators themselves satisfy nilpotence and cannot satisfy this Hecke quadratic relation for generic \(q\).',r'The exact Hecke presentation is revisited in record 726 and occurs in \citet[\S 1]{kerov1987realizations}; the notebook is juxtaposing related algebras, not identifying their unchanged generators.','the analogy is valid after specifying different operators or generators.',[K],reconstruction='The distinction between the nil-Coxeter operator and a Hecke generator prevents interpreting both quadratic relations simultaneously.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',area='Representation theory')
item(720,'P001402-P001403','construction','Schubert polynomials from dominant codes',r'A weakly decreasing code \(I\) is assigned the monomial \(x^I\); divided differences change adjacent entries by \((i_p,i_{p+1})\mapsto(i_{p+1},i_p-1)\) when \(i_p>i_{p+1}\).',r'For a permutation \(w\), let \(c_i(w)=\#\{j>i:w(j)<w(i)\}\). There is a unique stable family \(\mathfrak S_w\) with \(\mathfrak S_{w_0}=x_1^{N-1}x_2^{N-2}\cdots x_{N-1}\) in \(S_N\), and \(\partial_i\mathfrak S_w=\mathfrak S_{ws_i}\) when \(\ell(ws_i)<\ell(w)\), zero otherwise. If the code is weakly decreasing, \(\mathfrak S_w=x^{c(w)}\).',r'Lascoux--Sch\"utzenberger\textquotesingle s 1982 announcement is the pre-seminar source \citep{lascouxschutzenberger1982schubert}; the definition and code convention are read in \citet[\S 1]{kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert}. Earlier occurrence: 1985-661.7.','the construction is independent of a reduced word by the braid relations.', ['lascouxschutzenberger1982schubert','kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert'],reconstruction='The permutation code fixes the notebook\textquotesingle s indexing convention.',area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001403','construction','The worked chain from code 730 to code 025',r'The notes compute \(Y_{730}\to Y_{702}\to Y_{062}\to Y_{025}\) using \(\partial_2,\partial_1,\partial_2\) and complete-function determinants.',r'The divided-difference rules give \[\partial_2\partial_1\partial_2(x_1^7x_2^3)=Y_{025}=s_{(5,2)}(x_1,x_2,x_3)=h_5h_2-h_6h_1,\] where operator composition acts from right to left and each \(h_i\) uses the three-variable alphabet in the final equality.',r'The general rules are those of \citet[\S 1]{kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert} and Jacobi--Trudi \citep[Chapter I, (3.4)]{macdonald1995symmetric}. The concrete equality is checked by independent symbolic polynomial expansion in the accompanying script.','the displayed example is correct after maintaining the changing alphabets in the intermediate determinants.',[M,'kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert'],development='annotated',area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001404','theorem','Schur functions as special Schubert polynomials',r'For an increasing code \(0\le i_1\le\cdots\le i_n\) followed by zeroes, the notes identify \(Y_I\) with the Schur function previously defined.',r'If a permutation is Grassmannian with its only possible descent at \(n\), then \(\mathfrak S_w=s_\lambda(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\), where \(\lambda=(c_n(w),\ldots,c_1(w))\). Equivalently its first \(n\) code entries are weakly increasing and the later entries zero.',r'This is a classical specialization of the Schubert construction, recalled in \citet[\S 1]{kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert} and originating in the Lascoux--Sch\"utzenberger theory \citep{lascouxschutzenberger1982schubert}.','the identity explains why the previous worked example ends at an ordinary Schur function.', ['kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert','lascouxschutzenberger1982schubert'],area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001404','theorem','The finite Schubert basis and its cardinality',r'For \(n+1\) variables, codes satisfy \(0\le i_1\le n,\ldots,0\le i_{n+1}\le0\); the notes then give the dimension as \(n!\), despite listing six elements for \(n=2\).',r'For \(R_N=\mathbb Z[x_1,\ldots,x_N]/(e_1,\ldots,e_N)\), the classes \(\mathfrak S_w\), \(w\in S_N\), form a free integral basis, hence \(\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb Z}R_N=N!\). In the notebook\textquotesingle s \(N=n+1\) convention the count is \((n+1)!\).',r'The flag-cohomology basis is classical \citep[\S\S 2--3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert}. The count also follows independently by multiplying the \((n+1),n,\ldots,1\) choices for the code.','the basis assertion is correct; the printed factorial loses one in its argument.',[B],literal='false as written',development='annotated',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(720,'P001404','question','The symmetric-group action in the Schubert basis',r'Gelfand asks how variable permutation by \(S_n\) is written in Schubert polynomials.',r'The action on the coinvariant algebra is determined by \[s_i[P]=[P]-(x_i-x_{i+1})[\partial_iP].\] Thus apply \(\partial_i\mathfrak S_w\), multiply by the indicated linear form, and reduce in the finite Schubert basis. Over \(\mathbb Q\), the ungraded coinvariant representation is the regular representation of \(S_N\).',r'The Weyl-group action on flag cohomology is already a subject of \citet[\S 3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert}. The displayed operator formula follows immediately from the definition, so the recorded request is computational exposition rather than an unresolved existence problem.','the action is explicit; the graded pieces are not each regular representations.',[B],reconstruction='The quotient, the rational coefficient field for the regular representation, and the algorithmic meaning of an explicit formula are specified.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Representation theory')
item(720,'P001404-P001405','theorem','Poincaré pairing and adjoint divided differences',r'The pairing is obtained from \(\partial_1\partial_2\partial_1(PQ)\) in three variables; divided differences are self-adjoint, and a code-complement rule gives the dual basis.',r'On the coinvariant algebra for \(S_N\), define \((P,Q)=\epsilon(\partial_{w_0}(PQ))\), where \(\epsilon\) sets all variables to zero. Then \((\partial_iP,Q)=(P,\partial_iQ)\). With the standard Schubert indexing, \((\mathfrak S_u,\mathfrak S_v)=\delta_{v,w_0u}\); equivalently \(c_i(v)=N-i-c_i(u)\).',r'The pairing is integration on the flag manifold and the operators are its push-pull operators \citep[\S 3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert}. A separate finite-rank polynomial check tests the code complement and the adjoint identity.','the pairing is perfect over the integers, and evaluation after the longest divided difference is necessary for arbitrary degrees.',[B],reconstruction='The augmentation and indexing convention clarify the notebook\textquotesingle s phrase that the answer is a number in the quotient.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(720,'P001405','theorem','Freeness over symmetric polynomials',r'The polynomial ring in three variables is said to be free over its symmetric subring with Schubert-polynomial basis.',r'For \(N\ge1\), \(\mathbb Z[x_1,\ldots,x_N]\) is a free module over \(\mathbb Z[e_1,\ldots,e_N]\), with basis \(\{\mathfrak S_w:w\in S_N\}\). The basis has \(N!\) elements; it is not the entire stable family indexed by all finite permutations.',r'This is the polynomial form of the coinvariant-basis theorem \citep[\S 3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert}; the modern Schubert convention is fixed in \citet[\S 1]{kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert}.','the integral freeness statement holds with the finite indexing set made explicit.',[B,'kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(720,'P001405','problem','Schubert polynomials as characters',r'Gelfand requests a generalization of character theory whose characters are Schubert functions; the line also mentions irreducible representations without naming a group or category.',r'For each finite permutation \(w\), one can construct a finite-dimensional cyclic polynomial module \(S_w(E_\bullet)\) for the triangular group preserving a complete flag, whose diagonal-torus character is \(\mathfrak S_w\). It is generally not irreducible. A nonsymmetric Schubert polynomial cannot be the character of a polynomial \(\operatorname{GL}_N\)-representation, whose torus character is symmetric.',r'\citet[Theorem 4.1]{kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert} proves the exact Borel-module realization. Its introduction dates the preprint to 1986 and its announcement to 1987; the full article appeared in 2004. Thus this is class E at the seminar date, not a 2004 solution of a new 1987 problem.','the character realization is established in the Borel category; the notebook does not identify a category supporting its extra irreducibility wording.', ['kraskiewiczpragacz2004schubert'],reconstruction='The representation category is supplied by the matched theorem; irreducibility is explicitly not imported into it.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Representation theory')
item(720,'P001404','remark','A theory organizing determinant formulas',r'In discussing Muir\textquotesingle s determinant books, Gelfand proposes building a theory behind the formulas, by analogy with integrable systems.',r'The remark specifies neither a class of determinant identities, an invariant, nor a proposed equivalence. It supports a research programme, but no uniquely quantified conjecture or theorem.',r'The adjacent Schur and Schubert identities have exact classical matches. Searches for the Muir and integrable-system connection do not turn this unspecific sentence into a definite outstanding problem.','the programme remains too broad for a mathematical fate classification beyond F.',[],cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Integrable systems')
corr('P001399','matrix terminology','Hankel matrix','Upper triangular Toeplitz matrix','The displayed entry is h_(j-i), constant along diagonals, whereas a Hankel matrix depends on i+j. The scan confirms the heading.')
corr('P001399','alphabet with three variables','P=alpha u+beta u+gamma w','P=alpha u+beta v+gamma w','The generating product immediately below uses distinct u,v,w. The scan repeats u in the first line; this is a mathematical emendation.')
corr('P001404','dimension following the six-element example','n!','(n+1)! for the displayed n+1-variable convention','The explicit code bounds give (n+1)n...1 choices. The n=2 example lists the six S_3 Schubert classes.')
corr('P001405','character request','Irreducible representations with Schubert-function characters','Cyclic Borel modules give the established realization; the intended irreducible category is unspecified','Kraśkiewicz--Pragacz Theorem4.1 matches the character request but not irreducibility. Its introduction records 1986 preprint and 1987 announcement.',typ='ambiguity')
