talk(721,'1987-11-09','M. M. Kapranov','P001405-P001406','Double categories, two-categories, and higher fundamental groupoids',r'Kapranov introduces squares with horizontal and vertical composition, the structure now conventionally called a double category, and its interchange axiom. Kontsevich contrasts this with two-morphisms between parallel arrows. A tensor-contraction example, Gelfand\textquotesingle s boundary-value suggestion, and the homotopy squares of a filtered space give three distinct motivations.')
item(721,'P001405-P001406','definition','Double categories and the interchange law',r'The heading says 2-categories, while the data comprise objects, horizontal arrows, vertical arrows, and squares with two compositions.',r'A strict double category is a category object in categories: its squares have horizontal and vertical boundary arrows and associative unital compositions \(\circ_h,\circ_v\) satisfying \[(A\circ_vB)\circ_h(C\circ_vD)=(A\circ_hC)\circ_v(B\circ_hD)\] whenever the four squares have matching boundaries.',r'\citet[\S 1, equations (1.1)--(1.5)]{brownspencer1976double} provides these exact four kinds of data and the interchange law; the paper appeared in 1976.','this is established double-category terminology; the notebook uses a broader informal label.', ['brownspencer1976double'],reconstruction='The modern name separates this structure from the strict 2-category in the later intervention.',area='Category theory and topology')
item(721,'P001406','claim','Why naive corner-index contraction fails interchange',r'Four-index tensors are multiplied by contracting the two indices on a common edge; the note says this example does not satisfy interchange.',r'Write a square tensor as \(A(k,l;i,j)\). Define horizontal contraction by \((A\circ_hC)(k,r;i,s)=\sum_{j,l}A(k,l;i,j)C(l,r;j,s)\), and vertical contraction by \((A\circ_vB)(k,l;u,v)=\sum_{i,j}A(k,l;i,j)B(i,j;u,v)\). With indices \(0,1\), take the only nonzero entries of \(A,C,B,D\) to be, respectively, \(A(0,0;0,0)=C(0,0;0,0)=B(0,1;0,0)=D(1,0;0,0)=1\). The outer all-zero entry of \((A\circ_hC)\circ_v(B\circ_hD)\) is 1, while that of \((A\circ_vB)\circ_h(C\circ_vD)\) is 0.',r'The failure follows by two direct finite contractions, independently checked in the accompanying script. The relevant required axiom is \citet[equation (1.5)]{brownspencer1976double}.','the proposed naive contraction does fail; suitable categorical tensor constructions must keep different boundary data.', ['brownspencer1976double'],reconstruction='The full vertical contraction convention and sparse counterexample are ours; the notebook gives only a drawing of vertical composition.',match='partial',development='annotated',area='Category theory and topology')
item(721,'P001406','definition','Strict two-categories and Cat',r'Kontsevich proposes morphisms between morphisms with identical endpoints; Cat is the example. The note calls this a special case obtained by collapsing vertical arrows.',r'A strict 2-category has objects, 1-morphisms, and 2-morphisms between parallel 1-morphisms, with strictly associative unital horizontal and vertical compositions satisfying interchange. In \(\mathrm{Cat}\), these are categories, functors, and natural transformations. A strict 2-category embeds as a double category whose vertical arrows are identities.',r'This is pre-1987 higher-category formalism; \citet[\S 1 and references to B\'enabou, Gray, and Kelly--Street]{brownspencer1976double} distinguishes the double-category structure and its specializations.','the two descriptions are related, but their general definitions are not interchangeable.', ['brownspencer1976double'],speaker='M. L. Kontsevich',match='partial',area='Category theory and topology')
item(721,'P001406','remark','Harmonic boundary data as a categorical suggestion',r'Gelfand draws a square with \(\Delta u=0\), records \(u\) and \(\partial u/\partial n\) on its boundary, and asks Keldysh, Chentsov, and Gelfand to consider the idea.',r'The data suggest composing boundary-value relations by gluing domains. No spaces of admissible Cauchy data, corner conditions, morphisms, or composition law are defined, so an exact double-category theorem cannot be extracted.',r'The boundary-value and categorical keywords were checked against the surrounding record and the later higher-category thread. No precise publication can be matched to this fragment without choosing new analytic definitions.','this is a mathematically motivated programme but an indeterminate statement, not an identified unsolved analytic problem.',[],cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Category theory and analysis')
item(721,'P001406','construction','Homotopy squares of a filtered space',r'For \(X_0\subset X_1\subset X\), vertices lie in \(X_0\), edges are paths in \(X_1\), and squares map \((I^2,\partial I^2)\) to \((X,X_1)\), modulo homotopy.',r'For a filtered space \(X_0\subset X_1\subset X\), take paths in \(X_1\) with endpoints in \(X_0\), and squares in \(X\) whose edges lie in \(X_1\) and vertices in \(X_0\). Homotopies must preserve these filtrations and fix vertices; the resulting homotopy double-groupoid construction has horizontal and vertical gluing. At a basepoint, its relative structure is encoded by the crossed module \(\pi_2(X,X_1)\to\pi_1(X_1)\).',r'\citet[\S 1, Example 1; introduction; Theorem A]{brownspencer1976double} explains squares, their double compositions, and the crossed-module relation. Theorem A circulated in a 1972 preliminary report, before its 1976 publication.','the construction is classical once the homotopies and connections are specified; arbitrary unrelative homotopy would not preserve the necessary boundary data.', ['brownspencer1976double'],reconstruction='The filtered and vertex-fixed homotopy conventions are essential additions to the shorthand.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',area='Category theory and topology')
corr('P001405-P001406','heading and four kinds of data','2-category','Double category for the displayed data; the later parallel-arrow definition is a strict 2-category','Brown--Spencer1976 §1 defines precisely the objects, two arrow directions and squares shown here.',typ='ambiguity')
checkpoint()

talk(722,'1987-11-23','A. A. Beilinson','P001407','Derived categories: the cone lemma and roofs, revisited',r'This one-page continuation recalls the categories of complexes, homotopy classes, and derived morphisms. It states the cone proof of the Ore lemma and the equivalence relation on roofs. The outgoing quasi-isomorphism is written on the wrong leg of the opening diagram, an error visible in the original scan.')
item(722,'P001407','theorem','The cone lemma with its outgoing quasi-isomorphism',r'The initial roof has \(Z\to X\) a quasi-isomorphism; the outgoing diagram labels \(X\to Z_1\), rather than \(Y\to Z_1\), as a quasi-isomorphism.',r'For \(Z\xrightarrow{s}X\), \(Z\xrightarrow fY\), with \(s\) a quasi-isomorphism, there is a homotopy-commutative square with \(X\to P\) and a quasi-isomorphism \(Y\to P\). One may take \(P=\operatorname{Cone}((s,-f):Z\to X\oplus Y)\).',r'This repeats occurrence 1987-717.5 and the established multiplicative-system construction \citep[Lemma 13.11.2 and \S 13.5]{stacks2026foundations}. The wrong-leg version cannot be true for \(s=1_Z\) and an arbitrary map \(f\).','the correct lemma holds in the homotopy category; the original diagram requires an emendation.',[S,H],literal='false as written',development='annotated',area='Sheaves and homological algebra')
item(722,'P001407','construction','Homotopies, localization, and refinement of roofs',r'The page repeats \(f^i=dg^i+g^{i+1}d\), inverts quasi-isomorphisms in \(K(\mathcal A)\), and generates equivalence of roofs by precomposing with a quasi-isomorphism.',r'With complexes as objects, \(C(\mathcal A)\to K(\mathcal A)\to D(\mathcal A)\) first identifies homotopic chain maps and then inverts every quasi-isomorphism. A roof \((s,f)\) is equivalent to \((s\chi,f\chi)\) for a quasi-isomorphism \(\chi:Z_1\to Z\); the equivalence relation generated by these refinements is the derived-morphism relation.',r'\citet[Definition 13.11.3 and localization construction]{stacks2026foundations}; compare occurrences 1987-717.3--6 and earlier 1971-266.3.','this is a repeated foundational construction rather than a new problem or later development.',[S,H],area='Sheaves and homological algebra')
corr('P001407','opening Ore diagram','The outgoing arrow X to Z-prime is labelled quasi-isomorphism','The outgoing arrow Y to Z-prime is the quasi-isomorphism','The scan labels the left outgoing leg. Homotopy pushout along the incoming quasi-isomorphism Z to X makes the opposite leg Y to P a quasi-isomorphism.')
checkpoint()

talk(723,'1987-11-23','I. S. Zakharevich','P001408','Bernoulli coding, the baker map, and hyperbolicity',r'The short talk illustrates sensitivity to finite observational precision using the two-sided binary shift and the baker transformation. A closing comparison invokes geodesic flow on a negatively curved manifold. Measure-theoretic coding, topological conjugacy, and deterministic loss of exact information must be distinguished.')
item(723,'P001408','construction','The two-sided Bernoulli shift and baker map',r'The notes depict a bi-infinite sequence of zeroes and ones and encode the two halves as binary coordinates of a square.',r'Let \(\Sigma=\{0,1\}^{\mathbb Z}\) with product measure and \((\sigma a)_j=a_{j+1}\). Set \(x=.a_0a_1\cdots\), \(y=.a_{-1}a_{-2}\cdots\). Outside the null set of ambiguous dyadic expansions, this gives a measure-preserving conjugacy with \[B(x,y)=(2x-\lfloor2x\rfloor,(y+\lfloor2x\rfloor)/2).\]',r'The coding is checked directly by moving the binary point. The shift and hyperbolic examples belong to the pre-1987 theory surveyed in \citet[hyperbolic examples]{smale1967dynamical}; only that survey\textquotesingle s metadata was reopened in this run.','the measure-theoretic conjugacy is exact almost everywhere; the square and the Cantor product are not globally homeomorphic.', ['smale1967dynamical'],reconstruction='The formula, probability measure, and dyadic exception are supplied explicitly.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Dynamical systems')
item(723,'P001408','claim','Finite observation and loss of predictive information',r'Iterating the shift is described as gradually losing information about a point when only finitely many digits are known.',r'For independent fair binary coordinates, if only \(a_j\) with \(|j|\le m\) are observed, then any unobserved coordinate \(a_k\), \(|k|>m\), still has conditional probabilities \(1/2,1/2\). Under \(\sigma^n\), the observed window shifts, and future digits outside the initial finite window are not determined by it. The complete two-sided shift remains invertible.',r'This precise finite-window statement is a direct consequence of product independence, not a new theorem about destruction of exact state information. It clarifies the standard symbolic-dynamics example associated with \citet{smale1967dynamical}.','the observation-level assertion is correct; exact invertible dynamics does not erase its full initial data.', ['smale1967dynamical'],reconstruction='Conditional probability is our precise interpretation of the informal information claim.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Dynamical systems')
item(723,'P001408','claim','Negative curvature as a geometric hyperbolicity example',r'The closing line compares the binary example with geodesics on a negatively curved manifold.',r'If \(M\) is a closed smooth Riemannian manifold with strictly negative sectional curvature, its geodesic flow on the unit tangent bundle is Anosov: the tangent bundle splits into flow, stable, and unstable subbundles with uniform exponential contraction in forward or backward time.',r'\citet{anosov1967geodesic} is the original 1967 monograph; its Math-Net publication record was opened. The notebook does not state a literal conjugacy of this flow to a binary shift.','uniform hyperbolicity holds under compactness and negative curvature; arbitrary noncompact negatively curved manifolds need additional uniformity conditions.', ['anosov1967geodesic'],reconstruction='The closed-manifold hypothesis and the exact hyperbolicity assertion supply a limited rigorous interpretation.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='Dynamical systems')
checkpoint()

talk(724,'1987-11-23','G. A. Margulis','P001409-P001410','Rational iteration, Sullivan\textquotesingle s theorem, and fractal questions',r'Margulis moves from affine maps and squaring to the Fatou and Julia sets of a rational map, then sketches Sullivan\textquotesingle s quasiconformal proof excluding wandering domains. Gelfand\textquotesingle s interventions include circle linearization, elementary measure and dimension exercises, and a programme for discrete-group representations on fractal sets. The final disk-packing sketch omits the iterative rule needed for either residual-set question.')
item(724,'P001409','claim','Affine iteration and invariant circles',r'For \(z\mapsto az+b\), the notes say that \(|a|=1\) gives invariant circles carrying rotations.',r'If \(a\ne1\), translating by \(z_*=b/(1-a)\) conjugates the map to \(w\mapsto aw\). For \(|a|=1\), \(a\ne1\), the circles \(|z-z_*|=r\) are invariant. If \(a=1,b\ne0\), the map is a translation and has no fixed point or invariant Euclidean circle; if \(a=1,b=0\), it is the identity.',r'The correction follows by direct substitution and supplies the missing exceptional case in the recorded elementary example. The ensuing nonlinear setting is the rational dynamics of \citet[Introduction]{sullivan1985quasiconformal}.','the statement is valid after excluding the nonzero translation case.', ['sullivan1985quasiconformal'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',area='Complex dynamics')
item(724,'P001409','question','Smooth conjugacy of circle diffeomorphisms to rotations',r'Gelfand says the circle-conjugacy question is nontrivial and names Denjoy, Arnold, and Herman.',r'An orientation-preserving \(C^2\) circle diffeomorphism with irrational rotation number is topologically conjugate to the corresponding rotation. If it is \(C^\infty\) and its rotation number satisfies a Diophantine condition \(|\alpha-p/q|\ge Cq^{-2-\nu}\) for some \(C,\nu>0\) and all rational \(p/q\), the conjugacy can be chosen \(C^\infty\). Smoothness alone does not ensure a smooth conjugacy for every irrational rotation number.',r'\citet[Introduction and main theorem]{yoccoz1984conjugaison} proves the smooth Diophantine statement, generalizing Herman, in 1984. It predates this intervention.','the regularity and arithmetic qualifications are essential; the remark is about an established theory, not an explicitly new conjecture.', ['yoccoz1984conjugaison'],reconstruction='The modern formulation separates topological and smooth conjugacy, which the notes do not spell out.',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',match='partial',area='Dynamical systems')
item(724,'P001409','conjecture','Representations on sets of intermediate Hausdorff dimension',r'Gelfand asks for a representation theory of infinite discrete groups on functions on sets of intermediate Hausdorff dimension, suggesting that some classical theory might reappear.',r'The fragment specifies no class of groups, fractal spaces, measures, function spaces, actions, or meaning of recovering the classical theory. Consequently there is no definite universal existence or classification statement to test.',r'Targeted searches for Gelfand, representations of discrete groups, and Hausdorff dimension found related boundary and fractal representation constructions, but no publication with the quantifiers of an identifiable question here. Those thematic developments are not treated as a solution.','the programme remains indeterminate from this record; F records the missing formulation and does not assert that no such representations exist.',[],cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Representation theory and fractals')
item(724,'P001409','problem','A point outside short intervals around the rationals',r'The school-level question covers the nth rational by an interval of length \(\varepsilon/2^n\) and asks for a number left uncovered.',r'For any enumeration \((q_n)_{n\ge1}\) of \(\mathbb Q\) and open intervals \(I_n\ni q_n\) with \(|I_n|=\varepsilon2^{-n}\), \(m(\bigcup_nI_n)\le\varepsilon\). Hence for \(0<\varepsilon<1\), \([0,1]\setminus\bigcup_nI_n\) has measure at least \(1-\varepsilon\), is nonempty, and consists of irrationals. A particular uncovered number depends on the chosen enumeration and intervals.',r'This is countable subadditivity of Lebesgue measure, explicitly reconstructed here. The basic measure setting is described in \citet[\S 2.5]{hutchinson1981fractals}; no fixed numerical example can be inferred without the missing interval data.','the existence claim is elementary; the source does not specify enough data to name one universal uncovered real number.', ['hutchinson1981fractals'],reconstruction='The unit interval and epsilon bound turn the informal task into a precise existence assertion.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Measure and fractal geometry')
item(724,'P001409','problem','Hausdorff dimension of the Cantor set',r'Gelfand asks for the Hausdorff dimension of the perfect Cantor set, without spelling out its construction.',r'For the middle-thirds Cantor set \(C\), defined by \(C=\tfrac13C\cup(\tfrac23+\tfrac13C)\), \(\dim_HC=\log2/\log3\), and its Hausdorff measure in that dimension is finite and positive. An arbitrary perfect nowhere-dense subset of the line need not have this dimension.',r'\citet[\S 5.3(1), with the examples in \S 3.3]{hutchinson1981fractals} gives the self-similar dimension theorem. The paper appeared as a 1979 ANU report before its 1981 publication; this particular Cantor dimension is older still.','the standard middle-thirds exercise is settled; the construction must be specified to make the answer unique.', ['hutchinson1981fractals'],reconstruction='Middle thirds is the conventional interpretation, explicitly marked because the page only says Cantor perfect set.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Measure and fractal geometry')
item(724,'P001409','construction','Squaring and one-sided binary dynamics',r'For \(z\mapsto z^2\), the unit circle is invariant, the inside tends to zero, the outside to infinity, and the circle map resembles the one-sided baker map.',r'If \(|z|<1\), \(z^{2^n}\to0\); if \(|z|>1\), it tends to infinity on the Riemann sphere. On \(z=e^{2\pi i\theta}\), iteration is \(\theta\mapsto2\theta\pmod1\). Binary coding gives the one-sided shift apart from dyadic ambiguity; this map is two-to-one, unlike the invertible two-sided baker transformation.',r'The formulas follow directly from exponentiation. This same base example introduces \citet[p.~401]{sullivan1985quasiconformal}.','the dynamics is completely explicit and the one-sided qualifier is mathematically significant.', ['sullivan1985quasiconformal'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Complex dynamics')
item(724,'P001410','definition','The Fatou set and the Julia set',r'The Fatou set is the open set of local normality of the iterates, and its closed complement is the Julia set; the family is called compact.',r'For a rational map \(R:\widehat{\mathbb C}\to\widehat{\mathbb C}\) of degree at least two, \(F(R)\) consists of points with a neighborhood on which \(\{R^n:n\ge0\}\) is normal for locally uniform convergence in the spherical metric. \(J(R)=\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus F(R)\) is closed. Normal means relative sequential compactness in this topology, not that the set of iterates is itself closed and compact.',r'\citet[p.~403]{sullivan1985quasiconformal} states this stable-set definition and its relation to Fatou\textquotesingle s normal-family formulation.','these are the standard definitions with the spherical topology specified.', ['sullivan1985quasiconformal'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Complex dynamics')
item(724,'P001410','claim','An empty Fatou set requires the rational-map setting',r'The notes say the normality set may be empty, after beginning with polynomials and allowing rational functions more generally.',r'A rational map of degree at least two can have \(F(R)=\varnothing\), for example a Latt\`es map induced by an expanding endomorphism of an elliptic curve. A polynomial of degree at least two always has a nonempty basin of infinity and therefore a nonempty Fatou set.',r'The distinction is between the two ambient classes named in the record. The rational setting of \citet[Introduction]{sullivan1985quasiconformal} permits empty stable set; the polynomial exception follows from growth outside a sufficiently large disk.','the assertion is valid for rational maps, not for all maps in the narrower polynomial subcase.', ['sullivan1985quasiconformal'],reconstruction='The rational-versus-polynomial qualification is supplied explicitly; the example identifies the standard scope.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Complex dynamics')
item(724,'P001410','theorem','Sullivan: no wandering Fatou components',r'Sullivan is credited with showing that every component orbit stabilizes and that there are finitely many fixed components.',r'For every rational map \(R\) of degree at least two and every Fatou component \(U\), there exist integers \(m\ge0,p\ge1\) such that \(R^{m+p}(U)=R^m(U)\). There are finitely many periodic component cycles. Eventual periodicity does not mean that every component eventually becomes fixed under \(R\).',r'\citet[Theorem 1, p.~404]{sullivan1985quasiconformal} states eventual periodicity exactly; the following discussion treats the finite periodic types. The article appeared in September 1985, before the seminar.','the no-wandering-domain theorem is established; replacing periodic cycles by eventual fixed points overstates it.', ['sullivan1985quasiconformal'],literal='false as written',development='annotated',area='Complex dynamics')
item(724,'P001410','method','The quasiconformal deformation argument',r'The proof sketch supposes an infinite orbit of components and changes the conformal structures while keeping the map conformal.',r'The strategy is to assign compatible Beltrami data along a hypothetical wandering orbit, integrate it by the measurable Riemann mapping theorem, and obtain too many independent quasiconformal deformations for the finite-dimensional space of rational maps of the fixed degree. Producing the independent deformations and controlling conjugacy is part of the proof, not a consequence of parameter counting alone.',r'\citet[\S\S 7--10]{sullivan1985quasiconformal} implements the method. The introduction dates earlier lectures to 1978, 1980--81, and 1981; the final article is 1985.','the notebook accurately signals the proof mechanism but does not record a complete proof.', ['sullivan1985quasiconformal'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',area='Complex dynamics')
item(724,'P001410','problem','The area of a disk-packing residual set',r'A square filled with successively smaller tangent disks is drawn, with the instruction to inscribe them and prove that the remainder has measure zero.',r'Given a specified countable family of disjoint open disks \(D_j\subset Q\), the proposed conclusion would be \(m_2(Q\setminus\bigcup_jD_j)=0\). The notebook does not specify how the disks are chosen, which gaps must be filled, or a maximality or uniformity condition. Disjointness alone allows a positive-area remainder.',r'The scan shows only a tangency sketch and the word \ru{вписываем}. Searches for residual disk packings distinguish Apollonian and arbitrary packings; their theorems have different hypotheses. None can be identified as this exact unstated algorithm.','the intended packing process is indeterminate; no general zero-area theorem is asserted on the available data.',[],cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Measure and fractal geometry')
item(724,'P001410','problem','The dimension of the same packing remainder',r'The second question asks for Hausdorff dimension strictly below two.',r'For \(K=Q\setminus\bigcup_jD_j\), the requested claim is \(\dim_HK<2\). Even the additional condition \(m_2(K)=0\) would not imply it: zero measure and strict dimension deficit are different properties. The missing disk-selection rule prevents a uniquely specified conjecture.',r'No exact packing algorithm or quantitative scaling assumption was found in the supplied page or matched by targeted literature searches. Self-similar dimension results such as \citet[\S 5.3]{hutchinson1981fractals} require contraction data absent here.','the strict dimension bound cannot be classified as a definite open or solved problem without the omitted construction.', ['hutchinson1981fractals'],cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Measure and fractal geometry')
corr('P001409','affine map with unit multiplier','If absolute a=1, all points lie on invariant circles carrying rotations','Require a not equal to 1, or b=0; a=1,b nonzero is a translation','The scan explicitly writes absolute a=1. The affine fixed-point equation exposes the missing exceptional case.')
