talk(725,'1987-11-23','I. M. Gelfand','P001411-P001412','Matroids, bimatroids, and determinant relations',r'Gelfand revisits representability through the Fano configuration, basis exchange, and the nonzero minors of a matrix. Several questions are already classical, while the comparison with an uncertain named author and the request to discretize an entire syzygy chain lack a determinate target. The literal determinant identity contains several errors; a corrected exchange identity explains the intended argument.')
item(725,'P001411','definition','Vector matroids and symmetric basis exchange',r'The independent subfamilies of a finite vector configuration define its matroid. A displayed axiom permits simultaneous exchange in two bases.',r'Let \(E\) be finite. A nonempty family \(\mathcal B\) of equal-cardinality subsets is a matroid basis family if, for \(B_1,B_2\in\mathcal B\) and \(y\in B_2\setminus B_1\), there exists \(x\in B_1\setminus B_2\) such that both \(B_1-x+y\) and \(B_2-y+x\) belong to \(\mathcal B\). This symmetric exchange formulation is equivalent to the usual basis axiom. For a vector configuration, the bases are its maximal independent subfamilies.',r'This is classical matroid theory; the representation and independence definitions are stated in \citet[Theorem 1.6 and \S 1]{oxleybriefly}, while the basis-based bimatroid reformulation is recalled in \citet[Proposition 2.2]{rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids}. It repeats the subject of occurrence 1985-658.12.','the finite nonempty-family and equal-cardinality conventions make the displayed axiom precise.', ['oxleybriefly','rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids'],reconstruction='Nonemptiness, equal cardinality, and exclusion of the exchanged element from the other basis are explicit.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Matroid theory')
item(725,'P001411','problem','The Fano configuration and characteristic two',r'The seven-point diagram is to be realized over \(\mathbb F_2\), but not over \(\mathbb R\) or \(\mathbb C\).',r'The rank-three Fano matroid \(F_7\), whose lines are the dependent triples in \(\mathbb F_2^3\setminus\{0\}\), is representable over a field \(k\) if and only if \(\operatorname{char}k=2\). In particular its seven indicated incidence relations cannot all arise from a rank-three real or complex vector configuration with that matroid.',r'\citet[Theorem 4.1]{oxleybriefly} states the exact field criterion. The seven nonzero binary vectors give the recorded realization. This is the same exercise as 1985-658.14.','the problem is settled and is an expository obstruction to representability over the specified fields.', ['oxleybriefly'],area='Matroid theory')
item(725,'P001411','problem','Which finite matroids are real or complex representable?',r'The notebook calls the general question of representability over \(\mathbb R\) or \(\mathbb C\) unsolved, without specifying a type of classification.',r'For a finite rank-\(r\) matroid on \([n]\) with basis list \(\mathcal B\), representability over \(k=\mathbb R\) or \(\mathbb C\) is equivalent to the existence of \(A\in k^{r\times n}\) satisfying \(\det A_B\ne0\) for \(B\in\mathcal B\), and \(\det A_I=0\) for every other \(r\)-subset \(I\). These are finitely many polynomial conditions, and their solvability is decidable over both fields.',r'\citet[\S 2, pp.~15--42, and \S 3, pp.~43--46]{tarski1951decision} explicitly states quantifier elimination over the reals and its complex extension. The nonvanishing conditions can equivalently be encoded by an auxiliary equation \(u\prod_{B\in\mathcal B}\det A_B=1\). Thus the finite decision problem was settled before 1987. Occurrences 1985-658.13 and 1985-658.16 already connect representability with polynomial systems.','a finite decision procedure exists; the source specifies no stronger structural classification or complexity target that could responsibly be declared open.', ['tarski1951decision'],reconstruction='The finite-input decision interpretation is made explicit; it is not claimed to supply an efficient or illuminating combinatorial classification.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',area='Matroid theory')
item(725,'P001411','theorem','Matroids representable over every field',r'The notes say that representability over every field has been characterized.',r'For a finite matroid \(M\), the following are equivalent: \(M\) is representable over every field; \(M\) has a totally unimodular real matrix representation; and \(M\) has no minor isomorphic to \(U_{2,4}\), \(F_7\), or \(F_7^*\). Such matroids are called regular.',r'\citet[Lemma 4.4 and Theorem 4.5]{oxleybriefly} gives the exact equivalences and attributes them to Tutte 1958. The original Tutte paper was located but could not be opened in this run; the checked modern author exposition is therefore the immediate evidence.','the statement is an established characterization, with no new 1987 question recorded.', ['oxleybriefly'],verification='indirect',area='Matroid theory')
item(725,'P001411-P001412','definition','The bimatroid of nonzero minors',r'For a matrix with row set \(E\) and column set \(F\), the notes retain all pairs of equally sized subsets indexing a nonzero minor.',r'For a matrix \(A\in k^{E\times F}\), define \(\mathcal R(A)=\{(I,J):I\subseteq E,\ J\subseteq F,\ |I|=|J|,\ \det A_{I,J}\ne0\}\), including \((\varnothing,\varnothing)\) with determinant one. This records joint row-column dependence at every size, rather than just the row and column matroids.',r'\citet[Example 2.3]{rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids} gives this exact definition and recalls its origin in \citet{kung1978bimatroids}, published in 1978. The latter original remained inaccessible, so its attribution is indirect; the former result was read.','the construction was established before this seminar and has an exact distinguished-basis matroid encoding.', ['rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids','kung1978bimatroids'],area='Matroid theory')
item(725,'P001411-P001412','problem','An axiom system for bimatroids',r'The explicit question asks for axioms on the collection of nonzero square minors.',r'For finite disjoint \(E,F\), a collection \(\mathcal R\) of pairs \((I,J)\) with \(|I|=|J|\) is an abstract bimatroid exactly when \(\{(E\setminus I)\sqcup J:(I,J)\in\mathcal R\}\) is a matroid basis family on \(E\sqcup F\) containing \(E\). Thus nonemptiness, the pair \((\varnothing,\varnothing)\), and the ordinary basis-exchange axiom after this substitution are a complete axiom system. In the matrix case the extended matroid is represented by \([I_E\mid A]\).',r'\citet[Proposition 2.2 and Example 2.3]{rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids} proves this equivalence in the exposition of Kung\textquotesingle s 1978 theory. This is a later checked source for an older answer, not a 2026 solution date.','the recorded axiomatization problem is solved by an explicit exchange system; abstract bimatroids need not be realizable matrices.', ['rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids','kung1978bimatroids'],development='annotated',area='Matroid theory')
item(725,'P001412','question','The comparison with the uncertain named bimatroid theory',r'The page asks how a bimatroid notion in Gelfand--Serganova, UMN, relates to a name transcribed tentatively as \ru{Кунтом}.',r'An exact comparison would require two specified definitions and a map between their objects. The named passage in Gelfand--Serganova and the second surname are not identified unambiguously by the record. Kung is a plausible bibliographic lead, but this cannot determine the missing first definition.',r'The full English text of \citet{gelfandserganova1987combinatorial} was opened and searched; it develops ordinary and Coxeter matroids, but no occurrence of bimatroid identified the intended definition. \citet{kung1978bimatroids} is a relevant 1978 lead, with its modern formulation checked in \citet{rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids}. Neither fact proves the requested equivalence.','the comparison is indeterminate from the supplied citation and name; this is not a claim that the two theories differ or that an equivalence is unknown.', ['gelfandserganova1987combinatorial','kung1978bimatroids','rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids'],cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Matroid theory')
item(725,'P001412','problem','A determinant exchange identity and basis exchange',r'Equation (6) has a sign \((-1)^{d-i}\), repeats \(y_1\) in the first bracket, and inserts \(x_i\) into the second; the next sentence uses a nonzero summand to obtain exchange.',r'For \(x_1,\ldots,x_d,y_1,\ldots,y_d\in k^d\), with brackets denoting ordered determinants, \[[x_1,\ldots,x_d][y_1,\ldots,y_d]=\sum_{i=1}^d[y_i,x_2,\ldots,x_d]\,[y_1,\ldots,y_{i-1},x_1,y_{i+1},\ldots,y_d].\] If both left factors are nonzero, some summand is nonzero, proving simultaneous exchange of \(x_1\) with a suitable \(y_i\).',r'The corrected identity follows by expanding \(x_1\) in the \(y\)-basis and applying multilinearity; polynomial identity then removes the assumption that the \(y\)-matrix is invertible. It is part of the classical determinant relations recalled in \citet[\S 3, opening paragraph]{speyersturmfels2004tropical}. Direct symbolic checks give a counterexample to the literal notebook expression.','the intended exchange argument is valid after correcting the indices and the redundant sign.', ['speyersturmfels2004tropical'],reconstruction='The insertion positions are fixed exactly, so no additional alternating sign is needed.',literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='indirect',development='annotated',area='Algebraic geometry and combinatorics')
item(725,'P001412','claim','All relations among maximal minors',r'The notes claim that equation (6) supplies all relations among the determinant generators.',r'Let \(S=k[p_I: I\subseteq[n],\ |I|=r]\), and let \(S\to k[a_{ij}]\) send \(p_I\) to the corresponding maximal minor of a generic \(r\times n\) matrix. Its kernel is the Pl\"ucker ideal, generated by the full family of quadratic Pl\"ucker relations. One erroneous identity or one chosen instance does not by itself generate this ideal.',r'\citet[\S 3, opening paragraph]{speyersturmfels2004tropical} explicitly states the integral version and quadratic generation as a classical fact. The notebook\textquotesingle s assertion is correct only when (6) denotes the correctly indexed family of determinant exchanges, with the generic maximal-minor interpretation fixed.','the defining relations of the Grassmannian are established; the source formula requires the preceding emendation.', ['speyersturmfels2004tropical'],reconstruction='The generic matrix, coefficient ring, homomorphism, and full relation family are specified.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Algebraic geometry and combinatorics')
item(725,'P001412','problem','Writing the syzygy chain for a fixed Plücker ring',r'The requested Hilbert syzygy chain passes from determinant generators to relations and then to relations among relations; part (a) asks to write the whole chain.',r'For specified integers \(0<r<n\) and a field \(k\), the natural algebraic interpretation is a graded free resolution of \(S/I_{r,n}\) over \(S=k[p_I:|I|=r]\). A finite such resolution exists, with length at most \(\binom nr\), by Hilbert\textquotesingle s syzygy theorem. Finite polynomial presentations permit effective syzygy computation. The record does not specify minimality, characteristic, equivariance, or whether a uniform closed formula is required.',r'The finite-existence bound follows from \citet[Proposition 10.114.2, Tag 00OQ]{stacks2026foundations}. \citet[Chapter 7, \S 7.3]{weyman2003syzygies} specifically treats syzygies of Pl\"ucker ideals; the chapter description was checked, not a full resolution formula. Consequently neither that metadata nor the general theorem is presented as verification of a particular uniform chain intended by the speaker.','the finite existence and effective fixed-input task are settled; the stronger intended presentation is not specified well enough to assign it an open-problem status.', [S,'weyman2003syzygies'],reconstruction='A fixed generic Pluecker ring is a limited interpretation of the preceding determinant discussion, not a supplied source hypothesis.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',area='Commutative algebra')
item(725,'P001412','problem','Discretizing the whole syzygy chain',r'Part (b) asks to discretize the whole chain, with the gloss to write combinatorial relations.',r'No combinatorial objects, equivalence relation, coefficient theory, or criterion for recovering the algebraic syzygies is given. Thus the requested discretization is not a uniquely defined mathematical construction; the existence of a graded free resolution alone does not supply it.',r'The Pl\"ucker-syzygy literature located through \citet[\S 7.3]{weyman2003syzygies} addresses algebraic resolutions. Its metadata does not identify the extra combinatorial target in this sentence, and no exact publication match was found.','the omitted meaning of discretization prevents a definite solved or open classification.', ['weyman2003syzygies'],cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Commutative algebra')
item(725,'P001412','question','What information remains beyond the row and column matroids?',r'The notes suggest constructing a bimatroid from two matroids, then warn that a bimatroid contains additional information and ask what it is.',r'The pair of row and column matroids does not determine the bimatroid. Over \(\mathbb R\), \(I_2\) and \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\1&2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\) both have free row and column matroids of rank two, but the first has two zero \(1\times1\) minors and the second has none. The extra data are the compatible joint ranks \(\rho(I,J)=\operatorname{rank}A_{I,J}\), equivalently all nonzero square-minor pairs.',r'The counterexample is computed directly. \citet[Proposition 2.6]{rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids} gives the relative-rank characterization and its equivalence with the extended matroid. It also explains why separate row and column restrictions lose joint incidence data. The final cohomological motivation in the notes specifies no separate invariant or theorem.','the insufficiency of two matroids has an elementary witness and a precise joint-rank description.', ['rohrleulirsch2026bimatroids'],reconstruction='The two matrices and the joint-rank answer supply an explicit solution; the source does not contain this example.',match='partial',development='annotated',area='Matroid theory')
corr('P001411','general representability problem','Which matroids are real or complex representable is unsolved','The finite decision problem is decidable; a stronger intended structural classification is unspecified','Tarski1951 sections2 and3 state real quantifier elimination and extension to complex algebra; encode the finitely many required vanishing and nonvanishing maximal minors.',typ='ambiguity')
