talk(726,'1987-11-30','I. M. Gelfand','P001413-P001416','Kerov’s Hecke representations and Frobenius characters',r'Gelfand explicitly expounds Kerov\textquotesingle s work: Hecke generators, Young-tableau adjacency, and rational weights give an orthogonal-form construction. The seminar then asks for Hecke characters and recalls the ordinary Frobenius formula. The latter ends above the divider on P001416; the following Ruelle-zeta discussion belongs to record 727.')
item(726,'P001413','definition','The two presentations of the type-A Hecke algebra',r'Idempotents \(e_i\) satisfy the modified braid relation; the exercise changes to \(\sigma_i=(q+1)e_i-1\).',r'For \(q\in\mathbb C^\times\), \(q\ne-1\), put \(\tau=q/(q+1)^2\). The relations \(e_i^2=e_i\), \(e_ie_j=e_je_i\) for \(|i-j|>1\), and \(e_ie_{i+1}e_i-\tau e_i=e_{i+1}e_ie_{i+1}-\tau e_{i+1}\) become \((\sigma_i-q)(\sigma_i+1)=0\), the ordinary braid relation, and distant commutativity. The \(\sigma\)-presentation itself also makes sense at \(q=-1\).',r'\citet[\S 1, equations (1a)--(1c)]{kerov1987realizations} uses these exact generators. The change of generators is invertible only for \(q\ne-1\), a restriction hidden by the notebook\textquotesingle s parameter formula.','the two presentations agree on their stated common domain.', [K],reconstruction='Nonzero q and exclusion of minus one state the domain used by the cited paper; the standard presentation extends further.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Representation theory')
item(726,'P001413','problem','Dimension and symmetric-group specialization',r'At \(q=1\) the algebra is \(\mathbb C[S_N]\); the next exercise asks for \(\dim H_N(q)=N!\).',r'In the standard presentation, \(T_w=T_{i_1}\cdots T_{i_\ell}\) for a reduced expression \(w=s_{i_1}\cdots s_{i_\ell}\) is well defined, and \(\{T_w:w\in S_N\}\) is a basis of \(H_N(q)\). Consequently \(\dim H_N(q)=N!\). At \(q=1\), \(T_i^2=1\) and the algebra identifies with \(\mathbb C[S_N]\).',r'\citet[\S 1, p.~156]{kerov1987realizations} records the dimension and specialization in the already established theory. The same Hecke-basis topic occurs in 1979-483.2.','the dimension is independent of the parameter; semisimplicity is a separate issue.', [K],area='Representation theory')
item(726,'P001413','claim','The modular analogy and exceptional parameters',r'Dipper--James is recommended under the handwritten name Tipper; the theory is called analogous to modular symmetric-group representations, even over \(\mathbb C\), followed by the non-root-of-unity assumption.',r'For \(q\in\mathbb C^\times\), define \(e(q)\) as the least positive integer \(e\) with \(1+q+\cdots+q^{e-1}=0\), or infinity if none exists. Then \(H_N(q)\) is semisimple if and only if \(e(q)>N\). In the semisimple case its simple modules are indexed by partitions of \(N\); at exceptional roots of unity the modular analogy becomes substantive, and simples arise as nonzero simple quotients of Specht modules.',r'\citet[\S 1, p.~156]{kerov1987realizations} states this distinction and attributes it to \citet{dipperjames1986representations}. The publisher confirms January 1986 and pages 20--52; its full result was not read. Kerov\textquotesingle s full statement was read.','the generic hypothesis removes the nonsemisimple cases from the subsequent construction; complex coefficients alone do not ensure semisimplicity.', [K,'dipperjames1986representations'],reconstruction='The quantum characteristic makes the informal modular analogy precise without claiming that all generic cases are modular.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Representation theory')
item(726,'P001413-P001414','definition','Young diagrams, tableaux, and axial adjacency',r'Diagrams are partitions, written with column lengths. A tableau is an increasing chain of diagrams, and its axial distance compares the cells containing \(k\) and \(k+1\).',r'A standard tableau of shape \(\lambda\vdash N\) is a chain \(\varnothing=\lambda^{(0)}\subset\lambda^{(1)}\subset\cdots\subset\lambda^{(N)}=\lambda\), with one cell added each time. If cell \(k\) has row-column coordinates \((i_k,j_k)\), set \(r_k(t)=(i_{k+1}-j_{k+1})-(i_k-j_k)\), \(1\le k<N\). Say \(s\sim_k t\) when their chains agree except possibly at stage \(k\). For \(N\ge2\), the recorded axial parameter is \(h(\lambda)=1+\max_{t,k}|r_k(t)|\).',r'\citet[\S 2, p.~157]{kerov1987realizations} gives the same definitions with \(k<N\). The notebook\textquotesingle s upper limit \(k\le N\) refers to a nonexistent cell \(N+1\). Partitions also classify conjugacy classes of \(S_N\) by cycle lengths, but that labeling is distinct from the tableau construction.','the chain and axial-distance definitions are standard once the index range and row-column convention are fixed.', [K],literal='false as written',area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(726,'P001414','claim','The one- and two-tableau adjacency blocks',r'The two assertions distinguish axial distance \(\pm1\) from larger absolute value.',r'If \(|r_k(t)|=1\), then the only standard tableau \(s\sim_k t\) is \(t\). If \(|r_k(t)|>1\), there is exactly one other such tableau, obtained by interchanging \(k\) and \(k+1\), and it satisfies \(r_k(s)=-r_k(t)\). Thus each adjacency class has one or two elements.',r'\citet[\S 2, before Definition 1, p.~157]{kerov1987realizations} states this exact combinatorial fact. It follows directly from the increasing row and column conditions for a standard tableau.','the adjacency blocks are the elementary combinatorics behind the representation matrices.', [K],area='Algebraic combinatorics')
item(726,'P001414-P001415','construction','Kerov’s tableau matrices',r'The matrix entry is the square root of the product of two weights when \(s\sim_k t\), and zero otherwise; the theorem says these matrices represent \(H_N(q)\).',r'For \(q>0\), \(q\ne1\), and \(\lambda\vdash N\), define \(S^{(k)}_{tt}=W_{r_k(t)}\), \(S^{(k)}_{st}=\sqrt{W_{r_k(s)}W_{r_k(t)}}\) for the distinct adjacent tableau, and zero otherwise, taking the nonnegative square root. With the weights of the next item and \(\tau=q/(q+1)^2\), the assignment \(e_k\mapsto S^{(k)}\) satisfies the Hecke relations and realizes the corresponding Specht module. The limit \(q=1\) recovers Young\textquotesingle s orthogonal form. Generic complex parameters also work with coherent square-root choices.',r'\citet[\S 2, Definition 1, Theorem 1 and Remarks 1--2, pp.~157--158]{kerov1987realizations} proves the representation. The notebook expressly introduces this as a report on Kerov\textquotesingle s existing work. Its journal date is 1987; an exact earlier circulation day was not established.','this is an established construction already being expounded; arbitrary independent complex square-root choices should not be substituted for the coherent convention.', [K],reconstruction='The positive-real case supplies an unambiguous square-root convention; the broader generic-complex statement requires the convention in the original construction.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Representation theory')
item(726,'P001415','construction','The rational weights and their corrected identities',r'The recurrence starts at \(r\ge0\), despite \(W_0\) being undefined, and equation (19) says \(W_rW_{1-r}=1\). Explicit rational formulas follow.',r'For \(q\ne0,-1\) with the displayed denominators nonzero, and \(r\ge1\), \[W_r=\frac{q(q^{r-1}-1)}{(q+1)(q^r-1)},\qquad W_{-r}=\frac{q^{r+1}-1}{(q+1)(q^r-1)}.\] Then \(W_{-1}=1\), \(W_1=0\), \(W_r+W_{-r}=1\), and \(W_{-(r+1)}=1-\tau/W_{-r}\) for \(r\ge1\). For \(r\ge2\), \(W_rW_{1-r}=\tau\), not one. At \(q=1\) the limits are \(W_{\pm r}=(r\mp1)/(2r)\).',r'\citet[Appendix, equations (P1)--(P5), pp.~166--167]{kerov1987realizations} supplies the recurrence and product identity. Independent rational-function simplification confirms the factor \(\tau\) and the \(q=1\) limits.','the formulas are consistent after correcting the product constant and excluding the undefined zero index; poles require separate limiting representation theory.', [K],reconstruction='Initial values, valid integer ranges, and the removable q=1 singularity are stated explicitly.',literal='false as written',development='annotated',area='Representation theory')
item(726,'P001415','problem','A Frobenius formula for Hecke characters',r'After constructing representations, Gelfand asks to find the characters of the Hecke algebra, then turns to the ordinary symmetric-group formula.',r'In the ring of symmetric functions in \(X\), for generic semisimple \(H_N(q)\), normalized by \((T_i-q)(T_i+1)=0\), define \[Q_r(X;q)=\frac{1}{q-1}[t^r]\prod_j\frac{1-x_jt}{1-qx_jt},\qquad Q_\mu=\prod_aQ_{\mu_a}.\] If \(w_\mu\) is the product of consecutive disjoint cycles of lengths \(\mu_a\), then \[Q_\mu(X;q)=\sum_{\lambda\vdash N}\chi_q^\lambda(T_{w_\mu})s_\lambda(X).\] This gives generic irreducible character values on the standard cycle representatives; at \(q=1\), \(Q_r\to p_r\).',r'\citet{ram1991frobenius} published a precise Frobenius formula in 1991. The checked full formula is \citet[equation (4.10), p.~266, and Theorem 4.11]{halversonram1999bitraces}, after replacing their Hall--Littlewood \(q_r(X;q^{-1})\) by \(q^rq_r/(q-1)\). That paper explicitly credits several independent discoveries, including Vershik--Kerov; this classification makes no priority claim.','the generic Hecke-character problem has explicit symmetric-function formulas. Nonsemisimple root-of-unity simple characters are a different problem, not solved by this generic identity.', ['ram1991frobenius','halversonram1999bitraces'],reconstruction='The generic parameter, generator normalization, and cycle representatives make the broad character request precise.',cl='B',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',area='Representation theory')
item(726,'P001413-P001416','theorem','The ordinary Frobenius character formula',r'The opening task asks for symmetric-group characters; the closing alternant expands in power-sum products, with the coefficients equal to character values up to a simple constant.',r'For \(\lambda\vdash N\), \[s_\lambda=\sum_{\mu\vdash N}\frac{\chi^\lambda(\mu)}{z_\mu}p_\mu,\qquad z_\mu=\prod_{r\ge1}r^{m_r(\mu)}m_r(\mu)!,\qquad p_\mu=\prod_a p_{\mu_a}.\] Thus the omitted constant is the centralizer order \(z_\mu\). The alternant quotient is \(s_\lambda\) after reversing the notebook\textquotesingle s increasing exponent convention to \(\lambda_j+n-j\).',r'\citet[Chapter I, \S 7, especially equation (7.3) and the characteristic map]{macdonald1995symmetric} gives the precise Frobenius normalization. This is the classical result already cited in the notebook, not a 1987 invention.','the coefficients determine all irreducible symmetric-group characters, with partitions of total size N and the stated normalization.', [M],reconstruction='The total-degree condition and the exact centralizer factor replace the abbreviated coefficient description.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Representation theory')
